20260529.115028.210 [<ideas.........] ## Core assumptions in the current formal system
20260529.115028.217 [<ideas.........] The existing performance tests in `tests/test_enn_perf.py` and the optimizer CI gate in `tests/parity/parity_speed_gate.py`
20260529.115028.226 [<ideas.........] embed several implicit commitments:
20260529.115028.245 [<ideas.........] | Assumption | Extreme limit | Negation | Pathological edge |
20260529.115028.253 [<ideas.........] |---|---|---|---|
20260529.115028.500 [<ideas.........] | Measure one installed build | Only HEAD is timed; 9eaa27 is a memory, not an oracle in CI | Every assertion is a ratio of
20260529.115029.067 [<ideas.........] two builds in the same process on the same machine | Both builds slowed equally → tie-break ratios pass while rev regression
20260529.115029.086 [<ideas.........] is invisible |
20260529.115029.786 [<ideas.........] | Single-scenario spot check | One `(n, d, k)` tuple (e.g. 1024×10 self-search) | Timing must hold on a whole family of
20260529.115030.112 [<ideas.........] sizes simultaneously | Regression appears only at large `n` or only on the user-reported `n=20, d=1` path |
20260529.115030.885 [<ideas.........] | Independent repetitions → median | Warmup, then 8–12 isolated samples; report median | Each sample is a paired A/B draw;
20260529.115031.266 [<ideas.........] the statistic is over ratios, not over raw times | CI scheduler noise dominates; median of absolutes is flaky, median of
20260529.115031.372 [<ideas.........] paired ratios is not |
20260529.115031.944 [<ideas.........] | Timing the whole `posterior()` call | End-to-end wall clock is the signal | Decompose into phases and test the phase that
20260529.115032.831 [<ideas.........] changed | Neighbor lookup unchanged but posterior glue/regression path inflated—whole-call and tie-break A/B tests both miss
20260529.115032.841 [<ideas.........] it |
20260529.115033.540 [<ideas.........] | `tie_break_neighbors` is the axis of comparison | Tests compare tie-break on vs off on HEAD | Compare each flag value
20260529.115034.415 [<ideas.........] separately against its own 9eaa27 baseline; gate on `max(ratio_on, ratio_off)` | Slowdown is flag-independent; on/off ratio
20260529.115034.758 [<ideas.........] stays ≤1.15 while both are 2× slower than 9eaa27 |
20260529.115035.297 [<ideas.........] | In-process Python timing | `time.perf_counter()` around `model.posterior()` in pytest | Spawn a fresh interpreter per
20260529.115035.927 [<ideas.........] measurement; no shared allocator warmth | First-call penalty or import/JIT noise pollutes in-process medians |
20260529.115036.555 [<ideas.........] The prompt’s regression is specifically **HEAD slower than 9eaa27 regardless of `tie_break_neighbors`**. Tests that only
20260529.115036.866 [<ideas.........] compare tie-break on vs off on HEAD cannot detect that failure mode if both branches regressed together.
20260529.115036.886 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.115037.408 [<ideas.........] ## Idea 1: Paired alternation — the comparison unit is a ratio-per-rep, not a median-of-absolutes
20260529.115038.061 [<ideas.........] **Structural move:** Invert the statistic. Instead of `median(t_head)` divided by `median(t_9eaa27)`, run a single loop of
20260529.115038.794 [<ideas.........] paired draws: for each repetition, execute HEAD `posterior(x_train)` and 9eaa27 `posterior(x_train)` back-to-back (via `git
20260529.115039.620 [<ideas.........] worktree` subprocess or prebuilt wheel on `PYTHONPATH`), record `r_i = t_head_i / t_9eaa27_i`, and assert on `median(r_i)`
20260529.115039.747 [<ideas.........] (or trimmed mean of `r_i`).
20260529.115040.398 [<ideas.........] **Assumption pushed to the limit:** Scheduler and thermal noise is i.i.d. across reps. At the extreme, alternate
20260529.115041.041 [<ideas.........] HEAD/baseline on every iteration so each ratio shares the same CPU frequency, cache state, and CI jitter bucket. Collect
20260529.115041.667 [<ideas.........] 40–60 paired ratios, not 12 independent absolutes.
20260529.115042.062 [<ideas.........] **Negation explored:** No committed millisecond baseline JSON; the baseline is live 9eaa27 code in the same job. No
20260529.115042.393 [<ideas.........] warmup-then-measure independence; warmup is a single paired block, then all measured reps are paired.
20260529.115042.919 [<ideas.........] **Asymptotic / edge case:** For `tie_break_neighbors`, run the full paired loop twice (False and True) on frozen `np.savez`
20260529.115043.452 [<ideas.........] fixtures and assert `max(median(r_i | False), median(r_i | True)) ≤ 1.15`. This catches flag-independent slowdown because
20260529.115043.929 [<ideas.........] both branches are compared to their own 9eaa27 counterpart, not to each other on HEAD.
20260529.115044.577 [<ideas.........] **Why this is structurally distant:** The test’s primary random variable is a *distribution of ratios*, not a scalar wall
20260529.115044.974 [<ideas.........] time. The experimental design (paired blocks) changes; it is not a threshold tweak on `n` or `max_ratio`.
20260529.115045.018 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.115045.441 [<ideas.........] ## Idea 2: Curve-dominance gate — 9eaa27 commits a whole timing curve, not a point
20260529.115046.050 [<ideas.........] **Structural move:** Replace the single `n=1024` assertion with a **pointwise curve dominance** test. At 9eaa27 profiling
20260529.115048.548 [<ideas.........] time, record `{(n, median_ms)}` for `n ∈ {20, 64, 256, 1024, 4096}` on the fixed self-search fixture (`posterior(x_train)`
20260529.115048.608 [<ideas.........] with frozen data), separately for `tie_break_neighbors ∈ {False, True}`. Commit that dict to
20260529.115048.666 [<ideas.........] `tests/fixtures/posterior_speed_baseline.json` (same formalism as `optimizer_speed_baseline.json` but a curve, not one
20260529.115048.674 [<ideas.........] cell).
20260529.115048.681 [<ideas.........] The failing pytest computes the same curve on HEAD and asserts:
20260529.115049.131 [<ideas.........] - `median_ms_head(n, flag) / reference_ms_9eaa27(n, flag) ≤ 1.15` for **every** grid point, and
20260529.115049.740 [<ideas.........] - the discrete second difference in log-log space (curvature) at the largest `n` does not exceed 9eaa27’s curvature by more
20260529.115049.759 [<ideas.........] than ε.
20260529.115050.354 [<ideas.........] **Assumption pushed to the limit:** A regression is allowed at one `n` only if the whole curve stays dominated. At the
20260529.115051.030 [<ideas.........] extreme, a single-point pass is impossible: you must be faster-or-equal-ish across the entire committed surface.
20260529.115051.742 [<ideas.........] **Negation explored:** Not “is HEAD fast enough in absolute ms on this machine?” but “is HEAD’s *shape* no worse than
20260529.115052.424 [<ideas.........] 9eaa27’s shape?” Curvature catches superlinear blow-ups that a single-point test at `n=1024` can miss when small-`n`
20260529.115052.802 [<ideas.........] overhead masks large-`n` pathology.
20260529.115053.186 [<ideas.........] **Asymptotic / edge case:** Include `n=20, d=1` (user-reported scenario from `test_enn_batch_posterior_train_regression.py`)
20260529.115053.842 [<ideas.........] alongside batched self-search at `n=4096`. A neighbor-loop regression that is invisible at 1024 but catastrophic at 4096
20260529.115054.211 [<ideas.........] fails the curvature clause even if the 1024 ratio is 1.05.
20260529.115054.721 [<ideas.........] **Why this is structurally distant:** The oracle is a **two-dimensional surface** `(n, tie_break) → ms`, not one threshold.
20260529.115055.074 [<ideas.........] The assertion is geometric (dominance + curvature), not a single ratio.
20260529.115055.128 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.115055.893 [<ideas.........] ## Idea 3: Phase-subtraction with dual-build residual — time only what `posterior` adds above neighbor lookup
20260529.115056.538 [<ideas.........] **Structural move:** Decompose wall clock into two back-to-back calls on identical frozen `(train_x, train_y, query_x,
20260529.115056.547 [<ideas.........] params, flags)`:
20260529.115057.203 [<ideas.........] 1. `t_neighbor` = `enn_neighbor_distances_and_indices(...)` (FAISS batch neighbor path already used in `test_enn_perf.py`).
20260529.115057.449 [<ideas.........] 2. `t_posterior` = full `model.posterior(...)`.
20260529.115057.968 [<ideas.........] 3. `residual = t_posterior − t_neighbor` (same process, same rep, immediately adjacent).
20260529.115058.764 [<ideas.........] Run the paired-alternation harness from Idea 1 on **residuals**, not on full posterior time: compare `residual_head /
20260529.115059.278 [<ideas.........] residual_9eaa27` for each rep. Gate on `max` over `tie_break_neighbors ∈ {False, True}`.
20260529.115059.942 [<ideas.........] **Assumption pushed to the limit:** The entire neighbor-search stack is subtracted out. At the extreme, if 9eaa27 and HEAD
20260529.115100.625 [<ideas.........] spend identical time in FAISS/index but HEAD regressed in posterior glue (variance aggregation, batching, tie-break
20260529.115101.049 [<ideas.........] scaffolding that still runs, Python↔Rust marshalling), only the residual moves.
20260529.115101.689 [<ideas.........] **Negation explored:** Not timing the monolithic API surface; not comparing index_search vs FAISS (that is a different
20260529.115102.289 [<ideas.........] axis). The metric is “incremental posterior tax above neighbor lookup,” compared across revisions.
20260529.115102.993 [<ideas.........] **Asymptotic / edge case:** Run the residual gate on both the random Gaussian fixture (`n=1024, d=10`) and the lattice
20260529.115103.458 [<ideas.........] tie-heavy fixture already in `test_enn_perf.py`. If slowdown is irrespective of `tie_break_neighbors`, both flags must pass
20260529.115104.071 [<ideas.........] the residual ratio; if regression lives in shared neighbor code, residual stays flat and the test correctly does **not**
20260529.115104.798 [<ideas.........] fire—forcing you to pair with Idea 2’s curve on full `posterior` for neighbor-path regressions.
20260529.115105.352 [<ideas.........] **Why this is structurally distant:** The timed quantity is a **derived differential** between two existing internal
20260529.115105.901 [<ideas.........] operations, not `posterior()` alone and not a HEAD-only A/B between tie-break flags.
20260529.115105.949 [<ideas.........] ---
20260529.115106.006 [<ideas.........] ## tl;dr
20260529.115107.116 [<ideas.........] Three boundary-pushing pytest designs for detecting `posterior()` slowdown vs 9eaa27 in stable CI: **(1)** paired
20260529.115107.373 [<ideas.........] HEAD/9eaa27 alternation where each repetition yields a ratio and the test gates on the median of ratios, maxed over both
20260529.115108.021 [<ideas.........] `tie_break_neighbors` values; **(2)** a committed multi-`n` timing curve from 9eaa27 with pointwise ratio and log-log
20260529.115108.629 [<ideas.........] curvature dominance, not a single `n=1024` threshold; **(3)** phase subtraction (`posterior` minus neighbor lookup) compared
20260529.115109.257 [<ideas.........] across revisions so the test targets incremental posterior overhead rather than whole-call or tie-break-on/off HEAD
20260529.115109.511 [<ideas.........] comparisons.
